      "subsections": {
        "OVERVIEW": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "canonical_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Reflux of gastric or intestinal fluid into the esophageal lumen.",
              "pdf_page": 640,
              "printed_page": 558,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Incidence unknown; probably more common than clinically recognized.",
              "pdf_page": 640,
              "printed_page": 558,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Transient relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter (LES) or chronic vomiting may permit reflux of gastrointestinal juices into the esophageal lumen. Reflux is common in dogs with sliding hiatal hernias. A small amount of gastroesophageal reflux (GER) is a normal phenomenon in dogs and cats.",
              "pdf_page": 640,
              "printed_page": 558,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Gastric acid, pepsin, trypsin, bicarbonate, and bile salts are all injurious to the esophageal mucosa with prolonged or repetitive contact.",
              "pdf_page": 640,
              "printed_page": 558,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Sequelae of GER may vary from mild inflammation of the superficial mucosa to severe ulceration involving the submucosa and muscularis.",
              "pdf_page": 640,
              "printed_page": 558,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Systems affected include gastrointestinal (regurgitation) and respiratory (aspiration pneumonia).",
              "pdf_page": 640,
              "printed_page": 558,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
